+# browserctl
+
+`browserctl` is a single-track CLI for controlling a dedicated Chromium
+session.
+
+The first stage intentionally keeps the external interface simple:
+
+* `browserctl status`
+* `browserctl windows`
+* `browserctl tabs`
+* `browserctl page --limit 80`
+* `browserctl observe --limit 80`
+* `browserctl page-screenshot --full-page`
+* `browserctl eval "document.title"`
+* `browserctl activate-tab <tab-id>`
+* `browserctl focus`
+* `browserctl window --left 80 --top 40 --width 1280 --height 960`
+* `browserctl open <url>`
+* `browserctl navigate <url>`
+* `browserctl snapshot`
+* `browserctl screenshot`
+* `browserctl click --x <n> --y <n>`
+* `browserctl click-element --selector "#submit"`
+* `browserctl click-element --match-text "Login"`
+* `browserctl drag --start-x <n> --start-y <n> --end-x <n> --end-y <n>`
+* `browserctl scroll --x <n> --y <n> --notches-y <-3|3>`
+* `browserctl type "<text>"`
+* `browserctl type-element "hello" --selector "textarea" --clear`
+* `browserctl wait-selector "#app" --timeout-ms 5000`
+* `browserctl press ctrl+l`
+* `browserctl wait 500`
+* `browserctl daemon start`
+* `browserctl daemon stop`
+
+## Architecture
+
+* `browserctl.py` is the CLI entrypoint
+* `launcher.py` is the short human-facing wrapper for one-command use
+* `client.py` is the reusable Python client for CLI and MCP integrations
+* `browserd.py` is the local daemon
+* `cdp.py` adds page-aware control through the Chrome DevTools Protocol
+* `browserd` launches a dedicated Chromium profile with remote debugging
+  enabled
+* tab/session metadata comes from the DevTools HTTP endpoints
+* visible window actions currently reuse the existing
+  `agents/extensions/browser-control/server.py` implementation
+* `cdp.py` prefers `websocket-client` when available and falls back to a
+  built-in local `ws://` client so the core flow remains self-contained
+
+## Quick start
+
+```bash
+python tools/browserctl/launcher.py up
+python tools/browserctl/launcher.py observe
+python tools/browserctl/launcher.py mcp
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py daemon start
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py window --left 80 --top 40 --width 1280 --height 960
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py focus
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py status
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py navigate https://example.com
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py observe --limit 20
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py snapshot
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py page --limit 20
+python tools/browserctl/browserctl.py click-element --match-text "Sign in"
+```
+
+On Windows, the shortest wrapper from the repo root is:
+
+```powershell
+.\browser-agent.cmd up
+.\browser-agent.cmd observe
+.\browser-agent.cmd mcp
+```
+
+## Practical model
+
+* `open` creates a new tab when the DevTools helper endpoint is available
+* `navigate` behaves more like a human: it focuses the omnibox in the managed
+  window, types the URL, and presses Enter in the current tab
+* `window` lets you place the dedicated Chromium session in a predictable
+  location and size so repeated agent actions use a stable coordinate space
+* `browserd` serializes requests so one managed browser only executes one
+  action at a time, which matches the intended single-track control model
+* `page` returns a DOM-oriented summary of the visible page, including
+  interactive elements, text, selectors, labels, and bounds
+* `observe` combines a real visible-window screenshot with a CDP page summary
+  in one request, which is the most practical single call for agents
+* `click-element` and `type-element` use CDP + DOM lookup, so agents can act
+  on selectors or visible text instead of guessing screen coordinates
+
+## Agent recipe
+
+1. `browserctl observe --limit 40`
+2. Pick a target by `selector`, visible `text`, or `index`
+3. Use `click-element`, `type-element`, `wait-selector`, or `eval`
+4. Use `snapshot` for a real visible-window screenshot
+5. Use `page-screenshot` when you want a clean page capture without desktop
+   occlusion
+
+## Page commands
+
+* `page` inspects the current visible tab by default; pass `--tab-id` to force
+  a specific tab
+* `observe` returns both `snapshot` and `page`, so an agent can see browser
+  chrome and page DOM state in the same step
+* `activate-tab` switches the visible tab in the managed browser
+* `eval` is the escape hatch for advanced page reads and one-off DOM logic
+* `wait-selector` polls until a selector exists and is visible, unless
+  `--allow-hidden` is passed
+* `type-element` prefers editable controls and understands label text, so
+  `--match-text "Search"` can resolve an input associated with a `<label>`
+
+## Notes
+
+* This first stage is Windows-focused because visible window control reuses the
+  Windows-only browser-control layer.
+* The daemon uses a dedicated profile under `out/browserctl/profile`, so it
+  does not touch your default Chrome profile.
+* Agents can consume the same control surface through the `browserctl` MCP
+  extension under `agents/extensions/browserctl`.
